<!--
  CycloneDDS configuration for the ROSMASTER M1 (Raspberry Pi 5).

  Used by BOTH machines. Running the same RMW implementation on each side is
  not cosmetic: /tf_static is published with TRANSIENT_LOCAL durability, and
  that QoS is not reliably delivered across different DDS vendors. With the Pi
  on FastDDS and the operator PC on CycloneDDS, /tf arrived at 20 Hz but
  /tf_static never did - so slam_toolbox could not transform the scans and
  silently produced no map at all.

  ============================================================
  WHY UNICAST PEERS (this is the important part)
  ============================================================
  The Pi reaches the operator VM over Wi-Fi (wlan0). Wi-Fi sends multicast
  frames at the lowest basic rate, with no acknowledgement and no
  retransmission, so a lost multicast frame is simply lost.

  That asymmetry is invisible on small topics. /control_mode_state and
  /voltage are ~100 bytes at 1 Hz and arrived perfectly. A 640x480 JPEG is
  ~58 kB and is published 20 times a second - about 9.3 Mbit/s of multicast -
  and the operator PC received it at 2.6 Hz instead of 20 Hz. The camera
  looked broken; the network was.

  Setting AllowMulticast to "spdp" keeps multicast for participant discovery
  only (cheap, small, and it is what finds the peer in the first place), while
  all user data goes over unicast. Unicast over Wi-Fi is acknowledged and
  retransmitted, and here it runs at 433 Mbit/s - far more than the video
  needs.

  If either machine's address changes, update the Peer below on BOTH sides.

  SocketReceiveBufferSize is the second important setting. A large JPEG
  arrives as several UDP fragments and the DDS must hold all of them while
  reassembling. The kernel ceiling is raised separately
  (scripts/tune_network.sh); this asks Cyclone to actually use a large buffer.
  10 MB is comfortably inside the 16 MB net.core.rmem_max on this robot.
-->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config">
    <Domain id="any">
        <General>
            <!-- Naming the interface explicitly stops Cyclone from picking
                 docker0 (172.17.0.1) inside the host-network container, which
                 would make the robot invisible to the operator. -->
            <Interfaces>
                <NetworkInterface name="wlan0"/>
            </Interfaces>
            <!-- Discovery only. User data goes unicast to the Peer below. -->
            <AllowMulticast>spdp</AllowMulticast>
            <!-- Explicit "B" unit: a bare number is deprecated and warns on
                 every node start. 65500 B stays under a typical 64 KiB
                 datagram ceiling while leaving room for RTPS headers. -->
            <MaxMessageSize>65500B</MaxMessageSize>
        </General>
        <Discovery>
            <ParticipantIndex>none</ParticipantIndex>
            <Peers>
                <!-- The operator VM. -->
                <Peer address="10.0.0.102"/>
            </Peers>
        </Discovery>
        <Internal>
            <SocketReceiveBufferSize min="10MB"/>
        </Internal>
    </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
